      "sample_index": 6,
      "text": "Let's use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to solve this problem.\n\nWe are given that:\n- Andrew's collection has 17 albums.\n- John's collection has 6 albums that are not in Andrew's collection.\n- There are 9 albums that are in both collections.\n\nLet A be the set of albums in Andrew's collection and J be the set of albums in John's collection.\n\nWe want to find the number of albums in either Andrew's or John's collection, but not both.\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\n|A ∪ J| = |A| + |J| - |A ∩ J|\n\nwhere |A ∪ J| represents the number of albums in either Andrew's or John's collection, |A| represents the number of albums in Andrew's collection, |J| represents the number of albums in John's collection, and |A ∩ J| represents the number of albums in both collections.\n\nWe are given that |A| = 17, |J| = 6, and |A ∩ J| = 9.\n\nSo, we can plug these values into the equation:\n\n|A ∪ J| = 17 + 6 - 9 = 14\n\nTherefore, there are 14 albums in either Andrew's or John's collection, but not both.\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{14}.",
